## 8. Performance & Scalability Challenges

What happens if traffic scales up?

| Target Traffic | What Will Break | Architectural Solution |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **100 Active Users** | Nothing. The server easily handles this load. | Current architecture is sufficient. |
| **10,000 Active Users** | 1. **MongoDB Collection Scans**: Sorting high scores for the leaderboard without an index will slow down the database.<br>2. **Event Loop Throttling**: Express logging request times and saving scores to disk via Winston console logging will slow down the event loop. | 1. Add a MongoDB index to the high score field: `{ highScore: -1 }`.<br>2. Turn off console log colors and format Winston logs as JSON for faster execution. |
| **1,000,000 Active Users** | 1. **Leaderboard Bottleneck**: Fetching the leaderboard from MongoDB on every request will cause database CPU usage to hit 100%.<br>2. **JWT Storage (XSS)**: LocalStorage is vulnerable to XSS attacks, which could expose session tokens at scale. | 1. Move the leaderboard to **Redis** using a Sorted Set (`ZADD` / `ZREVRANGE`). This turns $O(N)$ database sorts into $O(\log N)$ in-memory lookups.<br>2. Move JWT tokens to secure, `httpOnly` cookies to protect them from XSS. |

